      "abstract": "Shared gaze helps re-establish joint attention in asymmetric remote\ncollaboration, yet existing MR systems source the expert’s gaze\nfrom a dedicated infrared eye tracker. We present CoGaze, which\nshares webcam-estimated expert gaze into a local worker’s MR\nview while an infrared reference records simultaneously, turning\nestimation noise into a measured variable. A four-participant pilot\nplaces that noise at 5.9° on average, with 95% of samples within 12.1°.\nWhether workers tolerate it remains untested: an instrumentation\ndefect left the displayed cue dominated by the infrared stream, so\nthe reduction in grounding cost we observed under gaze indicates\nthe value of gaze presence, not tolerance of webcam noise. We\ncontribute the system, the measured in-situ error bounds, a speechbased grounding measure sensitive where task accuracy was at\nceiling, and a successor design calibrated to those bounds.",
